finish('strong-exponential-time-hypothesis-deterministic',
 id='TCS-6595', title='Does the Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis hold?',
 area='Fine-grained complexity', criterion='tightness', question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''For every real ε∈(0,1), does there exist an integer k≥3 such that no deterministic algorithm decides k-SAT on n variables and input length L in time $O(2^{(1-\varepsilon)n}L^c)$ for any fixed constant c? Here k is fixed independently of n and L. This is the deterministic Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis (SETH).''',
 definitions=r'''A k-SAT input is an explicitly encoded conjunction of clauses, each a disjunction of at most k literals xᵢ or ¬xᵢ. Variables are relabeled consecutively and n counts the distinct variables actually present; L counts the full binary formula encoding, including literal indices and delimiters. The decision is exact satisfiability, with no promise about clause density, the number of satisfying assignments, or how the formula was generated. Empty clauses make the input unsatisfiable, and the empty conjunction is satisfiable. Running time is worst-case deterministic Turing-machine time. For a fixed k, the algorithm, polynomial exponent, and hidden constant may depend on k and ε, but not on the individual formula. Define sₖ=inf{α≥0 : k-SAT has an O(2^{αn}Lᶜ)-time deterministic algorithm for some constant c}. SETH equivalently asserts limₖ→∞sₖ=1; monotonicity follows because k-SAT is a restriction of (k+1)-SAT. Polynomial factors are retained explicitly so reading the input is allowed even when clauses are repeated. No advice, oracle, random bits, or quantum computation is allowed.''',
 answer_criterion='Prove the stated exclusion for every ε>0, or refute it by finding one fixed ε>0 such that every fixed width k has a deterministic O(2^{(1−ε)n}L^{c_k})-time algorithm. The algorithms may depend on k; the exponential saving ε must not. An improvement for only one width, or savings that tend to zero as k grows, do not refute SETH.',
 context=[
  paragraph('Exhaustive search tries all 2ⁿ assignments and checks the formula each time. Restricting clause width supports much faster algorithms, particularly for small k. SETH accommodates these speedups: it says that no positive constant saving in the exponent survives uniformly across arbitrarily large fixed widths. It does not assert that 3-SAT needs essentially 2ⁿ time.'),
  paragraph('The order of quantifiers separates this statement from several nearby claims. A 2^{0.9n}-time algorithm for every fixed k would refute SETH even if the polynomial factors and implementations depended on k. A family of bounds 2^{(1−1/k)n} would be compatible with it. A fast algorithm for unrestricted CNF-SAT would also refute it, but the failure of such an unrestricted algorithm is not the definition being used here.','book'),
  paragraph('ETH asks for a positive optimal exponential rate already at width three; SETH additionally asks that the rates approach one as width grows. Sparsification relates bounded-width formulas to bounded-density formulas and helps transfer these hypotheses through reductions. It does not make the two hypotheses equivalent. Proving either would go beyond P≠NP, which by itself allows intermediate running times such as 2^{√n}.','foundation'),
  paragraph('Fine-grained reductions turn this hypothesis into precise conditional barriers for familiar polynomial-time problems, including Orthogonal Vectors, edit distance, and graph-distance questions. The reduction must preserve the intended exponential saving, not merely be polynomial-time as in an NP-hardness reduction. When a reduction or resulting algorithm uses randomness, the corresponding randomized hypothesis must be tracked separately.','survey'),
  paragraph('Recent work continues to sharpen small-width algorithms. Jiang and Cai’s July 2026 preprint reports a slightly improved randomized PPSZ bound for general 3-SAT, distinguished from its better bound under a unique-solution promise. Both the fixed width and use of randomness keep this improvement outside the deterministic, all-width saving required to refute the present statement.','recent')],
 why='SETH is one of the main assumptions used to explain why specific polynomial running-time exponents resist improvement. Settling it would test the foundation of a broad network of tight conditional bounds in strings, graphs, data structures, and exact exponential-time algorithms.',
 importance=dict(score=99, method='editorial', assessed_on='2026-09-10', reason='A principal organizing hypothesis of fine-grained complexity, connecting exact SAT complexity to tight running-time barriers across many algorithmic areas.'),
 progress=[
  step('2001','Impagliazzo and Paturi study the optimal exponential rates for k-SAT and their growth with clause width, using critical clauses and sparsification.','foundation'),
  step('2010s','SETH-based reductions extend to numerous problems in polynomial time. Vassilevska Williams surveys the reductions, hypotheses, and resulting equivalence and hardness questions.','survey'),
  step('2015','The parameterized-algorithms textbook gives explicit deterministic definitions of ETH and SETH and separately discusses their randomized analogues.','book'),
  step('12 July 2026','Jiang and Cai report randomized O*(1.307031578ⁿ) time for general 3-SAT and O*(1.306969598ⁿ) for Unique-3-SAT by refining the analysis of PPSZ. These are preprint bounds for fixed width, not a constant saving valid for every k.','recent')],
 references=[
  ref('foundation','On the Complexity of k-SAT','Russell Impagliazzo; Ramamohan Paturi',2001,'https://cseweb.ucsd.edu/~paturi/myPapers/pubs/ImpagliazzoPaturi_2001_jcss.pdf','Journal of Computer and System Sciences 62(2), 367–375; rates sₖ and their limiting value; DOI 10.1006/jcss.2000.1727'),
  ref('book','Parameterized Algorithms','Marek Cygan; Fedor V. Fomin; Łukasz Kowalik; Daniel Lokshtanov; Dániel Marx; Marcin Pilipczuk; Michał Pilipczuk; Saket Saurabh',2015,'https://www.mimuw.edu.pl/~malcin/book/parameterized-algorithms.pdf','Section 14.1, Conjectures 14.1–14.2 and discussion on pages 469–470; Theorem 14.5; Section 14.5'),
  ref('survey','On some fine-grained questions in algorithms and complexity','Virginia Vassilevska Williams',2018,'https://people.csail.mit.edu/virgi/eccentri.pdf','ICM 2018 survey; Section 2 hypothesis conventions and Section 3 reductions from SETH; the survey often permits randomness, whereas this card fixes deterministic algorithms'),
  ref('recent','A Better Analysis For PPSZ For 3-SAT','Tao Jiang; Shaowei Cai',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.10697v1','12 July 2026 preprint; separate general and unique 3-SAT bounds; randomized PPSZ analysis')],
 status='source_open', status_note='The cited primary sources state SETH as an open hypothesis. Research through 10 September 2026 found no deterministic all-width constant-saving refutation or unrestricted proof. Bounds for a fixed width, random instances, restricted solvers, or unique satisfiability are not recorded as resolutions.', formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
